<title>USA: U.S. Mar producer prices fell 0.1 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Mar producer prices fell 0.1 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-04-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department monthly producer price index (1982 equals 100):</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:	 Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> -				 Mar    Feb	 Mar97/96</p>
<p> Finished Goods	     -0.1   -0.4		1.6</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	  0.4   -0.1		0.8</p>
<p> -Consumer Foods	     0.9   -0.3		2.5</p>
<p> -Energy Goods		-3.4   -1.2		3.5</p>
<p> Finished Goods Index-X  132.2  132.2		n/a</p>
<p>X-Data not seasonally adjusted. N/A-Not Available.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:	 Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> Finished Goods		Mar    Feb	 Mar97/96</p>
<p> -Gasoline		    -4.0   -3.1		7.6</p>
<p> -Heating Oil		 -9.9   -1.1	     -2.9</p>
<p> -Tobacco Products	   2.5   -0.2		5.1</p>
<p> -Passenger Cars	     0.2    0.2		0.2</p>
<p> -Capital Equipment	  0.3   -0.1		0.7</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	 -0.6   -0.1		0.6</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	 Unch   Unch		0.1</p>
<p> -Manufact Materials	-0.1   -0.1	     -0.1</p>
<p> -Construction Materials   0.3    0.4		2.9</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:	 Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	  Mar    Feb	 Mar97/96</p>
<p> -Intermed.Energy Goods   -4.6   -0.6		2.8</p>
<p> Crude Goods		  -6.9   -5.9		0.3</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy-X	0.6    1.0		0.1</p>
<p> -Food/Feedstuffs	    2.1   -1.9	     -1.9</p>
<p> -Nonfood Materials	-12.5   -8.3		1.9</p>
<p> -Energy Materials-Y     -19.2  -12.4		3.0</p>
<p> -Petroleum		  -13.4  -12.2	     -2.4</p>
<p>X-Excludes crude petroleum. Y-Includes crude petroleum. Note - Tobacco products and petroleum are not seasonally adjusted.  </p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast March producer prices to remain unchanged from last month. Excluding food and energy prices, they expected a 0.1 percent increase.  </p>
<p>The Department said the 3.4 percent drop in finished energy goods was the largest since a 3.5 percent decline in March 1991.</p>
<p>The March PPI decline of 0.1 percent was the third consecutive monthly fall. The last time this occurred was in May through August when it fell for four straight months.</p>
